@* A self-contained "Guide to Prayers" helper. Renders a floating circular button that stacks above the back-to-top control,
    plus a native <dialog>, so it is reachable on the prayers home and on every prayer page (covering shared links). It explains the versicle/response (V./R.) notation and
   how to pray a litany and a novena. *@

<FloatingGuide OrbKey="@OrbLauncher.Prayers" HideTrigger="HideTrigger" Slot="Slot" Title="Guide to Prayers" Eyebrow="A short orientation" AriaLabel="Guide to prayers">
    <Icon>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="-77 79 100 100" fill="currentColor"
             xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
            <path d="M-44.1,112.1c0-4.8,3.9-8.6,8.6-8.6c4.8,0,8.6,3.9,8.6,8.6c0,4.8-3.9,8.6-8.6,8.6C-40.2,120.7-44.1,116.8-44.1,112.1z
                     M-9.3,132.9h5.6V96.7h14.5v-0.2v-5.4v-0.2H-3.7V79h-5.6v11.8h-14.5l0,0.3H-24v5.6h14.7V132.9z
                     M-30.8,122.4c-2.2-1.2-5-0.4-6.2,1.8 l-2.4,4.4c-0.3-2.3-0.9-4.4-1.4-5.3c-1.2-2.5-2.8-3.5-2.8-3.5c-8.5-4.8-13,4.1-13.1,4.4c-0.8,2-2,5.2-1.9,11.6
                     c0.3,14.5,8.7,34.1,8.7,34.1h-16.3c-2.9,0-5.5,1.7-5.5,4.9c0,2.8,2.4,4.5,5.3,4.5l22.6,0c3.4,0,6-1.9,6.1-8.3l0.2-26.8l8.6-15.7 C-27.8,126.3-28.6,123.6-30.8,122.4z
                     M-30.9,143.8V179h5v-30.2h38.9V179h5v-35.2H-30.9z" />
        </svg>
    </Icon>
    <ChildContent>
        <p>
            Many of the Church's prayers follow patterns that are centuries old. Once you can
            read the little cues, you can pray any of them with confidence &mdash; alone or with others.
        </p>

        <GuideSection Title="Versicle and response">
            <p>
                Many prayers are built as a call and answer. Two small labels mark who says what:
            </p>
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem>
                    <TermContent><LiturgicalMark Symbol="BreviarySymbol.Versicle" Class="guide-term" /></TermContent>
                    <ChildContent>the <em>versicle</em>, the call, said by the leader.</ChildContent>
                </GuideItem>
                <GuideItem>
                    <TermContent><LiturgicalMark Symbol="BreviarySymbol.Response" Class="guide-term" /></TermContent>
                    <ChildContent>the <em>response</em>, the answer, said by everyone else.</ChildContent>
                </GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
            <p>
                Praying alone, simply say both lines yourself. Older books print them as V. and R.; the letters come from the Latin
                <em>versus</em> (verse) and <em>responsum</em> (response), and you will meet them
                throughout the Rosary, the Divine Office, litanies, and novenas.
            </p>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Title="Praying a Litany">
            <p>
                A <strong>litany</strong> is a prayer of many short invocations, each answered by the same
                repeated response. One voice names a title of the Lord, Our Lady, or a saint; everyone
                answers together. It is a steady, rhythmic way to pray, and the repetition is the point:
                it lets the heart rest on God while the words carry you along.
            </p>
            <GuideSteps>
                <GuideStep Cue="Begin with mercy">
                    Most litanies open with the <em>Kyrie</em> &mdash; &ldquo;Lord, have mercy. Christ, have mercy. Lord, have mercy.&rdquo;
                </GuideStep>
                <GuideStep Cue="Keep the one response">
                    Through the long list of invocations the answer stays the same &mdash; often
                    &ldquo;have mercy on us&rdquo; or &ldquo;pray for us.&rdquo; You can pray the responses in your heart if you are alone.
                </GuideStep>
                <GuideStep Cue="Close with the Lamb of God">
                    Litanies usually end with the threefold &ldquo;Lamb of God&rdquo; and a concluding prayer that gathers up everything asked for.
                </GuideStep>
            </GuideSteps>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Title="Praying a Novena">
            <p>
                A <strong>novena</strong> is a prayer repeated for <strong>nine days in a row</strong>. The
                number recalls the nine days the Apostles and Our Lady spent in prayer between the
                Ascension and Pentecost, waiting for the Holy Spirit. A novena is a way of asking
                perseveringly &mdash; of knocking until the door is opened.
            </p>
            <GuideSteps>
                <GuideStep Cue="Choose an intention">
                    Bring one clear need to the prayer and hold it before God each day.
                </GuideStep>
                <GuideStep Cue="Pray the same prayer daily">
                    Say the novena prayer once a day for nine consecutive days &mdash; a fixed time, such
                    as morning or before bed, makes it easier to keep.
                </GuideStep>
                <GuideStep Cue="Persevere to the ninth day">
                    If you miss a day, simply resume; what matters is the faithful, trusting repetition
                    over the nine days.
                </GuideStep>
            </GuideSteps>
        </GuideSection>
    </ChildContent>

    <Footer>
        <button type="button" class="btn" @onclick="context.CloseAsync">Begin praying</button>
    </Footer>
</FloatingGuide>


@code {
    [Parameter] public bool HideTrigger { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public int Slot { get; set; }
}
